window.MPX_INDEX=[{"t":"Introduction","p":"","u":"index.html","h":false,"b":"A modern ArduPilot ground control station for Linux, in the spirit of Mission Planner. Version 1.2.3 MPXAir documentation Plan, fly, set up and review ArduPilot drones, planes, rovers and boats from Ubuntu. It uses the same screens and files as Mission Planner, rebuilt as a fast, native Linux app. Install MPXAir Take the tour GitHub"},{"t":"What MPXAir does","p":"Introduction","u":"index.html#what-mpxair-does","h":true,"b":"\u2708\ufe0f Fly HUD, live map, quick view, actions, and Air and Ground status. \ud83d\uddfa\ufe0f Plan Missions, geofence, rally points, survey grids, read and write. \ud83d\udee0\ufe0f Setup Firmware, calibrations, flight modes, failsafes, motor test. \ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f Parameters Every setting, grouped, searchable and explained. \ud83d\udcc8 Logs Download, graph and replay flights. \ud83d\udce1 Ground station Your own GPS position, Follow Me and antenna tracking."},{"t":"Why MPXAir","p":"Introduction","u":"index.html#why-mpxair","h":true,"b":"Familiar. The same workflow as Mission Planner, with the same .waypoints and .param files, parameter descriptions and mission commands. Native and modern. GTK 4 and libadwaita, light and dark styles, small-screen and touch friendly. Self-contained. No downloads from outside sources. Everything it needs ships with Ubuntu. The MAVLink protocol, map engine, log reader and firmware uploader are all part of MPXAir. Ground station aware. It reads the GPS of your ground station (for example an RFD900 + ESP32 board) and shows the aircraft and the ground station side by side."},{"t":"Where to start","p":"Introduction","u":"index.html#where-to-start","h":true,"b":"Install MPXAir , or just run it from its folder. Take the quick tour with the built-in demo drone. You don't need any hardware. Connect your aircraft over USB, a telemetry radio, UDP or TCP. Check the setup and calibration pages before the first flight. Tip Press / anywhere on this site to search."},{"t":"Installing and running","p":"","u":"install.html","h":false,"b":"Run MPXAir straight from its folder, or install it so it appears in Ubuntu's app grid."},{"t":"Requirements","p":"Installing and running","u":"install.html#requirements","h":true,"b":"Ubuntu 24.04 or newer. It is developed and tested on Ubuntu 25.10. Other Linux desktops with GTK 4.12+ and libadwaita 1.4+ also work. Python 3.10 or newer and these Ubuntu packages, which a standard Ubuntu desktop normally already has: python3-gi , python3-cairo , gir1.2-gtk-4.0 , gir1.2-adw-1 . Optional: gir1.2-geoclue-2.0 , to use the computer's location service as the ground position."},{"t":"Get MPXAir","p":"Installing and running","u":"install.html#get-mpxair","h":true,"b":"git clone https://github.com/narl3yyy-svg/MPXAir.git cd MPXAir Or download the ZIP from the GitHub page ( Code \u2192 Download ZIP ) and unpack it."},{"t":"Run it without installing","p":"Installing and running","u":"install.html#run-it-without-installing","h":true,"b":"./run.sh Command What it does ./run.sh Start MPXAir. Settings go in ~/.config/mpxair , missions and logs in ~/Documents/MPXAir . ./run.sh --portable Keep settings, map cache and logs inside the folder ( userdata/ ). Good for a USB stick you carry between computers. ./run.sh --connect or mpxair --connect Start and connect straight away to the last-used device. ./run.sh --demo Run only the demo drone in the terminal ( tcp:127.0.0.1:5790 ). Useful for testing other ground stations. If something is missing, run.sh says exactly which packages to install."},{"t":"Install it","p":"Installing and running","u":"install.html#install-it","h":true,"b":"./install.sh It checks the Ubuntu packages and offers to install any that are missing with apt . Only then does it ask for your password. It copies MPXAir to ~/.local/share/mpxair . It adds an MPXAir launcher to the app grid and an mpxair terminal command. It offers to add you to the dialout group, which is needed for USB flight controllers and radios. Log out and back in afterwards. To install for every user on the computer, use ./install.sh --system , which installs to /opt/mpxair ."},{"t":"Update","p":"Installing and running","u":"install.html#update","h":true,"b":"cd MPXAir git pull ./install.sh Your settings, maps and logs are kept."},{"t":"Uninstall","p":"Installing and running","u":"install.html#uninstall","h":true,"b":"./uninstall.sh Use ./uninstall.sh --system for a system-wide install. Your settings ( ~/.config/mpxair ), map cache ( ~/.cache/mpxair ) and logs ( ~/Documents/MPXAir ) are left in place. USB access If MPXAir says Permission denied when opening a USB port, run sudo usermod -aG dialout $USER , then log out and back in."},{"t":"Quick start tour","p":"","u":"quick-start.html","h":false,"b":"Try every screen in five minutes with the built-in demo drone. No hardware needed."},{"t":"Connect to the demo drone","p":"Quick start tour","u":"quick-start.html#connect-to-the-demo-drone","h":true,"b":"Start MPXAir. At the top right, open the connection list and pick Demo drone . Press Connect . A progress window shows the link opening, the heartbeat and the parameters loading, just like Mission Planner's connection window. The connection window counts the parameters as they arrive."},{"t":"Fly it","p":"Quick start tour","u":"quick-start.html#fly-it","h":true,"b":"Open Fly . On the Actions tab, press Takeoff , enter 20 m and confirm. The demo drone switches to GUIDED, arms and climbs. Right-click the map and choose Fly to here . Watch the HUD, the quick-view tiles and the trail. Press RTL to bring it home."},{"t":"Plan a mission","p":"Quick start tour","u":"quick-start.html#plan-a-mission","h":true,"b":"Open Plan and press Takeoff in the side panel. Click the map three or four times to add waypoints, then press RTL . Press Write . A window shows each item being sent and confirmed. Back on Fly \u2192 Actions , press Start Mission ."},{"t":"Look around","p":"Quick start tour","u":"quick-start.html#look-around","h":true,"b":"Setup \u2192 Motor test shows the motor layout of the demo's quad. Parameters : pick a group on the left, for example Battery (BATT) , and hover over a row to see what it does. Logs \u2192 Download from vehicle lists the demo's logs. Ground : the demo also pretends to be a ground station with its own GPS. Choose A ground station on the telemetry link to see it on the map. Note The demo drone is a simple pretend aircraft for learning the screens. For realistic testing, use the ArduPilot simulator ."},{"t":"Connecting to your aircraft","p":"","u":"connecting.html","h":false,"b":"USB, telemetry radios, UDP and TCP \u2014 and what the connection window tells you."},{"t":"Choose a connection","p":"Connecting to your aircraft","u":"connecting.html#choose-a-connection","h":true,"b":"The connection controls are at the top right of the window: the connection list, a refresh button, the baud rate or address, and Connect . Connection Use it for Settings ACM0 \u00b7 \u2026 , USB0 \u00b7 \u2026 A flight controller on USB, a telemetry radio, or a ground station board Baud rate: 115200 for USB flight controllers, usually 57600 for radios UDP listen Wi-Fi telemetry, companion computers, other software forwarding MAVLink Port, usually 14550 TCP The ArduPilot simulator, network bridges host:port , e.g. 127.0.0.1:5760 UDP send Sending to a fixed address host:port Demo drone Trying MPXAir without hardware \u2014 USB devices are listed with a short name, for example ACM0 \u00b7 Espressif . Open the list to see the full name. New devices are noticed as soon as they're plugged in."},{"t":"The connection window","p":"Connecting to your aircraft","u":"connecting.html#the-connection-window","h":true,"b":"When you press Connect , a window shows each step, like Mission Planner's: Opened the port or address. Heartbeat received from the aircraft, with its type and system ID. This is the proof the air unit is talking to you. Parameters counting up, for example 812 / 1431 . Mission , Geofence and Rally points read from the aircraft. It closes on its own a couple of seconds after everything has loaded. Press Hide to close it early (loading carries on), or Cancel while it is still waiting for the heartbeat. Heartbeat received and all 1431 parameters loaded."},{"t":"Ground station only (aircraft off)","p":"Connecting to your aircraft","u":"connecting.html#ground-station-only-aircraft-off","h":true,"b":"If you connect to a ground station board (for example the RFD900 + ESP32 board ) while the aircraft is switched off, MPXAir keeps the link open and says Waiting for aircraft . The ground station's GPS is shown straight away. When the aircraft powers up, MPXAir connects to it and loads its parameters automatically."},{"t":"Disconnecting","p":"Connecting to your aircraft","u":"connecting.html#disconnecting","h":true,"b":"Press Disconnect (top right). A window asks you to confirm, because you'll lose telemetry and control of the aircraft from MPXAir. If the aircraft is armed , the warning says so, and reminds you that its ground-station failsafe may trigger. After you confirm, MPXAir closes the port completely, stops Follow Me and any automatic reconnect, and confirms Disconnected \u2014 /dev/ttyACM1 is free for other programs . You can then open the port in another program, such as the GCS Settings page or Mission Planner. One program at a time While MPXAir is connected it has the serial port to itself, and other programs get a \"busy\" error. If another program already has the port open when you press Connect, MPXAir tells you which one."},{"t":"Automatic reconnect","p":"Connecting to your aircraft","u":"connecting.html#automatic-reconnect","h":true,"b":"If a USB device drops off (unplugged, or a power dip on the board), MPXAir shows Reconnecting\u2026 and waits up to two minutes. It recognises the same device by its unique USB ID, even if it comes back under a new name (for example ttyACM1 instead of ttyACM0 ), then reconnects and reloads everything. Press Stop to give up. It never reconnects after you press Disconnect yourself."},{"t":"Telemetry recording","p":"Connecting to your aircraft","u":"connecting.html#telemetry-recording","h":true,"b":"Everything received while connected is saved to a telemetry log ( .tlog ) in ~/Documents/MPXAir/telemetry-logs . You can replay it later. Turn this off in Preferences \u2192 General."},{"t":"If it doesn't connect","p":"Connecting to your aircraft","u":"connecting.html#if-it-doesn-t-connect","h":true,"b":"No heartbeat within 30 seconds. Check that the aircraft is powered, that the right port is selected, and that the baud rate matches the radio (57600 for most SiK/RFD radios). Permission denied. Add yourself to the dialout group (see Troubleshooting ). The port isn't in the list. See a USB device disappears ."},{"t":"Fly screen","p":"","u":"fly.html","h":false,"b":"Everything you need while the aircraft is flying: the HUD, the map, live numbers and quick actions. The Fly screen with the aircraft (AIR) and the ground station (GROUND) on the map."},{"t":"HUD","p":"Fly screen","u":"fly.html#hud","h":true,"b":"The artificial horizon at the top left works like Mission Planner's: Horizon and pitch ladder : the aircraft's roll and pitch. Heading tape along the top. A green marker shows the bearing to the current target. Speed tape (left) and altitude tape (right, height above home). A climb-rate bar is on the right edge. ARMED / DISARMED in the middle, plus NO TELEMETRY if the link drops. Along the bottom: flight mode, battery, GPS, distance to the waypoint, link quality and the time. Warnings in orange or red: VIBE (vibration), EKF (position estimate) and PREARM (not ready to arm)."},{"t":"The map","p":"Fly screen","u":"fly.html#the-map","h":true,"b":"AIR : the aircraft, as a red arrow when armed, with a heading line and a target-bearing line. GROUND : your ground station , as a blue antenna badge with its satellite count. A dashed line joins it to the aircraft, labelled with the distance. H : home. The mission, geofence and rally points are drawn too. Trail : where the aircraft has been. It keeps the last 20 seconds by default and older track fades out. Change this with Trail on map keeps on the Actions tab, or in Preferences. Aircraft GPS card (bottom-left), as in Mission Planner: fix type, satellites and HDOP, green, orange or red by quality. Ground GPS card (bottom-right): the ground station's fix, satellites, HDOP and distance to the aircraft. If the ground GPS loses its fix, the GROUND marker stays at its last known position, greyed, with \"no fix \u00b7 last \u2026 ago\". Status chips (top): armed state, mode, altitude and speed, battery and link quality. The aircraft's GPS card (bottom-left) and the ground station's GPS card (bottom-right)."},{"t":"GPS fix types","p":"Fly screen","u":"fly.html#gps-fix-types","h":true,"b":"Shown as Meaning No GPS / No Fix No GPS detected, or not enough satellites yet. 2D Fix Position but no reliable altitude. Not good enough to fly in position modes. 3D Fix A full position. The minimum for Loiter, RTL and missions. 3D Fix + DGPS A 3D fix improved with correction signals from SBAS satellites (WAAS in North America, EGNOS in Europe, and so on). This is better than a plain 3D fix, and it's normal to see it for the whole flight. RTK Float / RTK Fixed Centimetre-level positioning with an RTK base station. The fix type alone isn't everything. Also check the satellites (10 or more is good) and HDOP (below 1.5 is good). ArduPilot refuses to arm with PreArm: High GPS HDOP until the HDOP is low enough."},{"t":"Map buttons (right side)","p":"Fly screen","u":"fly.html#map-buttons-right-side","h":true,"b":"Button What it does Follow Keeps the map centred on the aircraft. Ground station Shows or hides the ground station on the map. Keep both in view Frames the aircraft and the ground station together and follows both. Zoom + / \u2212 Zoom. The scroll wheel and touchpad pinch work too. Map type Satellite, street, topographic\u2026 and offline maps . Fit mission Shows the whole mission."},{"t":"Right-click menu","p":"Fly screen","u":"fly.html#right-click-menu","h":true,"b":"Fly to here (guided), Fly to here at altitude\u2026 , Point camera here , Set home here , Measure distance from here , Centre map here , Set ground station position here and Copy coordinates ."},{"t":"Quick view","p":"Fly screen","u":"fly.html#quick-view","h":true,"b":"Big, easy-to-read numbers. You can have 2 to 16 tiles. Click a tile to choose what it shows, from 45 values in sections: height and position, speed and attitude, navigation, battery, GPS and health, telemetry link, and ground station. Add a tile is at the bottom. Remove this tile is in each tile's menu. Useful extras include Distance flown , Power (W) , Fade margin , Radio RSSI (aircraft) , Distance / bearing / elevation from ground and Vibration ."},{"t":"Actions","p":"Fly screen","u":"fly.html#actions","h":true,"b":"Arm / Disarm. If arming is refused, MPXAir shows the aircraft's reason and offers Force Arm for experts. Takeoff. Switches to GUIDED, arms and climbs to the height you enter. Planes switch to TAKEOFF mode instead. Mode buttons for the common modes, plus a list of every mode. Mission : Set WP , Start Mission , Restart Mission . Guided changes : change speed and altitude. Vehicle : calibrate the gyro or barometer, reboot, trigger the camera; Set Home Here , Clear Track , and the trail length."},{"t":"Status","p":"Fly screen","u":"fly.html#status","h":true,"b":"Air status covers the vehicle, armed and ready, battery, GPS, EKF, vibration, telemetry link, SiK radio RSSI and noise, home, flight time, wind and firmware. Ground status covers your position and its source, the aircraft's distance, bearing and elevation from you, the laptop battery, map server and internet status, telemetry recording, data rates, Follow Me and the antenna tracker. Each row has a green, orange or red dot."},{"t":"Messages and RC/Servo","p":"Fly screen","u":"fly.html#messages-and-rc-servo","h":true,"b":"Messages lists everything the autopilot says, colour-coded (errors in red, warnings in orange). Important errors also pop up as a notification. RC/Servo shows the 16 radio inputs and 16 outputs as live bars."},{"t":"Plan screen","p":"","u":"plan.html","h":false,"b":"Create missions, geofences and rally points on the map, and send them to the aircraft. A mission with leg distances, the list of items, and the item editor."},{"t":"Make a mission","p":"Plan screen","u":"plan.html#make-a-mission","h":true,"b":"Set New item altitude and the Altitude reference in the side panel. Press Takeoff to add a takeoff (only one is allowed; pressing it again selects it). Click the map to add waypoints. Drag a waypoint to move it. Finish with RTL (return to launch) or Land . Press Write . A window shows each part being sent and what the aircraft confirmed. The bottom-left card shows the number of items, the total distance and an estimated flight time. It says not written to vehicle until you press Write."},{"t":"Right-click on the map","p":"Plan screen","u":"plan.html#right-click-on-the-map","h":true,"b":"Waypoint, spline waypoint, loiter (turns / time / until told otherwise), land here, point camera here (ROI), set the planned home."},{"t":"Selecting and editing","p":"Plan screen","u":"plan.html#selecting-and-editing","h":true,"b":"Click an item in the list (or its marker) to select it. Ctrl+click adds or removes items, and Shift+click selects a range. The editor shows only the fields that command uses, with the same names as Mission Planner. Hover over a field to see what it means. Changing a waypoint to Takeoff removes its map position (a takeoff climbs straight up). Changing it back puts it next to the previous waypoint. With several items selected, you can set the altitude of all of them at once. Move items up or down, and delete with Delete . Undo with Ctrl + Z and redo with Ctrl + Shift + Z ."},{"t":"Altitude reference","p":"Plan screen","u":"plan.html#altitude-reference","h":true,"b":"Reference Meaning Relative to home Height above the takeoff point. This is the usual choice. Above sea level Absolute altitude (AMSL). Above terrain Height above the ground below. Needs terrain data on the aircraft."},{"t":"Common commands","p":"Plan screen","u":"plan.html#common-commands","h":true,"b":"Command What it does Waypoint Fly to a point. Delay waits there for some seconds. Spline Waypoint Fly a smooth curve through the point. Takeoff Climb to the altitude. Loiter Turns / Time / Unlim Circle or hover for a number of turns, for some seconds, or until told otherwise. Return To Launch Go home (and land, for copters). Land Land at the point (or where it is). Do Change Speed Change speed for the rest of the mission. Do Set ROI Point the camera or gimbal at a location. Do Jump Repeat part of the mission."},{"t":"Geofence","p":"Plan screen","u":"plan.html#geofence","h":true,"b":"Switch to Geofence at the top of the map. Click to add the corners of an inclusion polygon (the aircraft must stay inside). Right-click for an exclusion polygon (keep out), inclusion or exclusion circles , or the return point . Settings\u2026 turns the fence on or off and sets the maximum altitude and the breach action. Remember Writing the fence shape doesn't switch the fence on. Use Settings\u2026 (FENCE_ENABLE)."},{"t":"Rally points","p":"Plan screen","u":"plan.html#rally-points","h":true,"b":"Switch to Rally and click to add safe places for RTL to go instead of home."},{"t":"Survey grid","p":"Plan screen","u":"plan.html#survey-grid","h":true,"b":"Switch to Survey area and click the corners of the area. Press Generate grid\u2026 and choose the line spacing, direction, altitude and turn margin. MPXAir adds back-and-forth waypoints covering the area."},{"t":"Clearing","p":"Plan screen","u":"plan.html#clearing","h":true,"b":"The bin button on the map toolbar can clear the mission, geofence, rally points, survey area or everything . Each mode also has a Clear button in the side panel. When connected, you can choose Clear plan (the aircraft keeps its copy until you Write) or Clear plan and vehicle (erase the aircraft's copy straight away)."},{"t":"Read, write and files","p":"Plan screen","u":"plan.html#read-write-and-files","h":true,"b":"Read gets the mission, geofence and rally points from the aircraft, with a progress window. Write sends them and shows what was confirmed, for example Mission: 5 items written and confirmed . The Files button loads and saves .waypoints files (the Mission Planner format) and .poly survey areas."},{"t":"Installing firmware","p":"","u":"firmware.html","h":false,"b":"Put ArduPilot on a flight controller from ArduPilot's official firmware list, or from your own .apj file. Before you start Use a USB cable straight to the flight controller, not a telemetry radio. Don't unplug it while it's being written."},{"t":"Install from ArduPilot's list","p":"Installing firmware","u":"firmware.html#install-from-ardupilot-s-list","h":true,"b":"Open Setup \u2192 Install firmware . Choose the Vehicle type (Copter, Plane, Rover, Sub, Helicopter, Antenna tracker, Blimp) and the Release : Stable ; Beta ; Latest (development) . Press Detect board . If you're connected, MPXAir restarts the board in bootloader mode. Otherwise, plug the board in within 30 seconds. The board ID is filled in for you. Press Find firmware , select a build, and press Install selected firmware . MPXAir downloads, erases, writes and verifies the firmware (with a CRC check), then reboots the board. Wait about 10 seconds and connect again."},{"t":"Install your own file","p":"Installing firmware","u":"firmware.html#install-your-own-file","h":true,"b":"Press Load a .apj file\u2026 and choose an ArduPilot .apj firmware file. MPXAir checks that it matches the board before writing."},{"t":"How it works","p":"Installing firmware","u":"firmware.html#how-it-works","h":true,"b":"MPXAir speaks the same bootloader protocol as Mission Planner and ArduPilot's uploader.py . The firmware list comes from firmware.ardupilot.org/manifest.json.gz and is cached for 24 hours. Tested The uploader was tested against a simulated bootloader with a 1.6 MB CubeOrange image, verified byte for byte. Test it on a board you can recover the first time."},{"t":"Frame and calibration","p":"","u":"calibration.html","h":false,"b":"Frame type, accelerometer, compass and radio calibration \u2014 do these before the first flight."},{"t":"Frame type","p":"Frame and calibration","u":"calibration.html#frame-type","h":true,"b":"Setup \u2192 Frame type sets FRAME_CLASS and FRAME_TYPE (or the QuadPlane Q_FRAME_* settings) and the board orientation. A diagram shows the motor layout of the selected frame. Reboot after changing the frame class."},{"t":"Accelerometer","p":"Frame and calibration","u":"calibration.html#accelerometer","h":true,"b":"Press Calibrate accelerometer . MPXAir asks for six positions in turn: level, left side, right side, nose down, nose up and on its back. Hold the vehicle still and press Done \u2014 next position each time. When it says Calibration successful , reboot the autopilot. Also available: Calibrate level only (trims roll and pitch with the vehicle level) and Simple calibration (one position, for large vehicles)."},{"t":"Compass","p":"Frame and calibration","u":"calibration.html#compass","h":true,"b":"Move away from metal, cars and magnets. Press Start . Slowly turn the vehicle so each side points at the ground in turn. The bars show progress for each compass. When all compasses say Success , press Accept if asked, then reboot. Lower fitness numbers are better. Below the calibration are the compass settings (use, orientation, fitness)."},{"t":"Radio","p":"Frame and calibration","u":"calibration.html#radio","h":true,"b":"Switch the transmitter on. You should see the bars move. Press Calibrate and move every stick and switch to both ends. Centre the sticks, put the throttle down, and press Done . Check the values and press Save ."},{"t":"Flight modes, failsafes and battery","p":"","u":"modes-failsafes.html","h":false,"b":"What each switch position does, what happens when something goes wrong, and how much battery is left."},{"t":"Flight modes","p":"Flight modes, failsafes and battery","u":"modes-failsafes.html#flight-modes","h":true,"b":"Setup \u2192 Flight modes lists the six positions of your mode switch with their PWM ranges. Choose a mode for each. The row for the current switch position is highlighted live, so flick the switch to check. Copters also have Simple and Super simple boxes. Press Save modes ."},{"t":"Failsafes","p":"Flight modes, failsafes and battery","u":"modes-failsafes.html#failsafes","h":true,"b":"Setup \u2192 Failsafe groups the settings for: Radio (throttle) failsafe : what to do when the RC signal is lost. Battery failsafe : low and critical voltage or mAh, and the actions. Ground station failsafe : lost telemetry. EKF failsafe : a bad position estimate. Return to launch : RTL altitude, final altitude, loiter time, speed. The live line at the top shows the throttle input and warns if it is below the failsafe value."},{"t":"Battery monitor","p":"Flight modes, failsafes and battery","u":"modes-failsafes.html#battery-monitor","h":true,"b":"Set the sensor type, capacity, pins and scaling. Each setting shows its full value under its name, and hovering shows what it does."},{"t":"Calibrate the voltage","p":"Flight modes, failsafes and battery","u":"modes-failsafes.html#calibrate-the-voltage","h":true,"b":"Measure the battery with a multimeter. Enter the reading in Measured voltage and press Apply measured voltage . MPXAir corrects BATT_VOLT_MULT so the reported voltage matches."},{"t":"Motor test","p":"","u":"motor-test.html","h":false,"b":"Check every motor is on the right output and spins the right way, using a diagram of your actual frame. Remove the propellers Always take the propellers off before testing motors. Quad Plus: big numbers are outputs, letters are test buttons, and arrows show the spin direction."},{"t":"Reading the diagram","p":"Motor test","u":"motor-test.html#reading-the-diagram","h":true,"b":"The front of the vehicle is at the top (yellow arrow). The big number is the output (servo) the motor must be plugged into. The letter is the motor-test button. Letters go clockwise from the front. The arrow shows the spin direction looking from above: green is clockwise, blue is counter-clockwise. The diagram comes from ArduPilot's motor layout data for your FRAME_CLASS and FRAME_TYPE . Coaxial frames (Y6, OctaQuad) show the top and bottom motors side by side. A coaxial Y6 frame."},{"t":"Testing","p":"Motor test","u":"motor-test.html#testing","h":true,"b":"Set the throttle (about 5\u201310 %) and duration. Press a letter. That motor lights up in the diagram and should spin. If a different motor spins, its wire is on the wrong output. If it spins the wrong way , swap any two of its three motor wires (or reverse it in the ESC settings). Test all in sequence spins each motor in turn. Stop all stops at once."},{"t":"Parameters","p":"","u":"parameters.html","h":false,"b":"Every setting on the aircraft, grouped, searchable and explained \u2014 and changed safely."},{"t":"Finding a parameter","p":"Parameters","u":"parameters.html#finding-a-parameter","h":true,"b":"Groups (left): browse by subject, such as Battery (BATT) , Failsafes (FS) , Servo / motor outputs (SERVO) and EKF3 (EK3) , without knowing the exact name. Small groups are under Other . Search : type part of the name or of the description, such as rtl alt or battery capacity . Searching always looks at every parameter. Filter : All, Changed (not yet written), Standard or Advanced. Hover over any row to see what it does."},{"t":"Changing parameters","p":"Parameters","u":"parameters.html#changing-parameters","h":true,"b":"Select a parameter. The editor on the right shows its description, units, range and a control: a dropdown for named values, switches for bitmasks, or a number field. Press Keep change to collect it (changed values are highlighted), or Write now to send it straight away. Press Write changes at the top to send everything you collected. If any parameter needs a reboot, MPXAir offers to do it."},{"t":"Files","p":"Parameters","u":"parameters.html#files","h":true,"b":"Save\u2026 writes every parameter to a .param file (Mission Planner format). Load\u2026 reads a Mission Planner, MAVProxy or QGroundControl file. It compares it with the aircraft and stages only the differences for you to review. Calibration and counter values are never overwritten."},{"t":"Descriptions","p":"Parameters","u":"parameters.html#descriptions","h":true,"b":"Select a parameter to see, on the right: What it does : the full description; Options : what each value means, with the current one marked (or the bits, for bitmasks); its range, units and step, and whether it needs a reboot. When you connect while online, MPXAir downloads the descriptions that exactly match the aircraft's firmware version (for example ArduCopter 4.7.1), as Mission Planner does. They are cached, so they also work offline later. Without internet it uses the bundled descriptions. Preferences \u2192 Telemetry \u2192 Update downloads ArduPilot's latest set by hand."},{"t":"Logs","p":"","u":"logs.html","h":false,"b":"Download the aircraft's flight logs, graph anything in them, and replay recorded flights."},{"t":"Download from the vehicle","p":"Logs","u":"logs.html#download-from-the-vehicle","h":true,"b":"Logs \u2192 Download from vehicle \u2192 List logs shows the logs on the aircraft's SD card. Select some and press Download selected , or Download all . Missing pieces are requested again automatically. Logs are saved in ~/Documents/MPXAir/flight-logs . Erase all\u2026 clears the SD card."},{"t":"Review a log","p":"Logs","u":"logs.html#review-a-log","h":true,"b":"Open .bin log\u2026 (or open one straight after downloading). Quick graphs : Attitude, Altitude, Battery, Vibration, GPS, Throttle. Or tick any field in the list on the left. Use the filter box to find messages. Scroll to zoom, drag to pan and double-click to reset. Hover to read the values. Flight-mode changes are marked. Flight path shows the GPS track on the map. Messages and Parameters show what was in the log."},{"t":"Replay a flight","p":"Logs","u":"logs.html#replay-a-flight","h":true,"b":"Logs \u2192 Replay telemetry plays back a .tlog recording on the Fly screen: the HUD, map and status exactly as they were. Use the bar at the bottom of the map to play and pause, seek, change the speed (0.5\u00d7 to 25\u00d7) or stop."},{"t":"Maps and offline maps","p":"","u":"maps.html","h":false,"b":"Satellite and street maps, saved for flying where there's no internet."},{"t":"Map types","p":"Maps and offline maps","u":"maps.html#map-types","h":true,"b":"Map Best for Satellite (Esri World Imagery) Seeing the real terrain Satellite + labels Satellite with place names and roads Street map (Esri) Roads and buildings Topographic (Esri) Terrain and contours OpenStreetMap Detailed community map OpenTopoMap Hiking-style terrain map Change the map with the map-type button on the Fly or Plan map, or in Preferences \u2192 Maps."},{"t":"Offline maps","p":"Maps and offline maps","u":"maps.html#offline-maps","h":true,"b":"While online, move the Fly or Plan map to where you'll fly. Open the menu (top left) and choose Offline Maps\u2026 . Choose the map type, the area (what the map shows, or 1, 3 or 10 km around it) and the zoom range. The estimate shows the number of tiles and the size. Press Download . The area is saved in the list. Later, press Refresh on a saved area (or Refresh all ) to download newer maps when you're back online. Note OpenStreetMap and OpenTopoMap don't allow bulk downloading, so they can't be saved as areas. Tiles you view in them are still cached."},{"t":"Internet use","p":"Maps and offline maps","u":"maps.html#internet-use","h":true,"b":"Preferences \u2192 Maps \u2192 Internet use : Online : download maps as you view them, and refresh saved tiles older than N days. Saver : only download tiles you don't have yet. Offline : never use the internet."},{"t":"Preferences and shortcuts","p":"","u":"preferences.html","h":false,"b":"Appearance, defaults, maps, telemetry rates, and keyboard shortcuts."},{"t":"General","p":"Preferences and shortcuts","u":"preferences.html#general","h":true,"b":"Style : follow Ubuntu, light, or dark. Default waypoint altitude and default takeoff altitude . Flight trail on the map : 10 s \u2026 5 min, or the whole flight. Record telemetry logs ( .tlog )."},{"t":"Maps","p":"Preferences and shortcuts","u":"preferences.html#maps","h":true,"b":"Map type, internet use, the refresh age for saved tiles, and a link to Offline Maps. See Maps ."},{"t":"Telemetry","p":"Preferences and shortcuts","u":"preferences.html#telemetry","h":true,"b":"How often the aircraft sends each kind of data (attitude, position, status, sensors, radio channels). Lower these on slow radio links. They take effect on the next connection. Here you can also update the parameter descriptions."},{"t":"Keyboard shortcuts","p":"Preferences and shortcuts","u":"preferences.html#keyboard-shortcuts","h":true,"b":"Keys Action Alt + 1 \u2026 Alt + 6 Switch screens (Fly, Plan, Setup, Parameters, Logs, Ground) Ctrl + , Preferences Ctrl + Z / Ctrl + Shift + Z Undo / redo in Plan Delete Delete the selected plan items Ctrl + Q Quit"},{"t":"Ground position","p":"","u":"ground-position.html","h":false,"b":"Tell MPXAir where you and your antenna are. It's used for the GROUND marker, distances, Follow Me and antenna tracking."},{"t":"Where the position comes from","p":"Ground position","u":"ground-position.html#where-the-position-comes-from","h":true,"b":"On the Ground tab, choose one of: Source Use it when A ground station on the telemetry link Your ground station has its own GPS and shares the USB/serial connection with the aircraft, for example an RFD900 + ESP32 board . Then choose its type . Separate USB GPS receiver A GPS plugged in on its own port, sending NMEA. Choose the port and baud rate. This computer's location Ubuntu's location service (Wi-Fi or a built-in GPS). Turn on Location in Ubuntu's Settings \u2192 Privacy. A fixed position you type in A ground station that doesn't move. Use the vehicle's home fills it in for you. Not used MPXAir doesn't need to know where you are. The Status row shows the position and how it is being read. The choice takes effect straight away. Press Apply after editing a port or coordinates."},{"t":"Seeing it in MPXAir","p":"Ground position","u":"ground-position.html#seeing-it-in-mpxair","h":true,"b":"Fly map : the blue GROUND badge with its satellite count, and a dashed line to the aircraft with the distance. Keep both in view frames them together. GND GPS chip (top of the Fly map): satellites and distance to the aircraft. Quick view : Distance / Bearing / Elevation from ground and Ground GPS satellites . Status \u2192 Ground status : position, source, and the aircraft's distance, bearing and elevation from you. Automatic When MPXAir finds an RFD900 + ESP32 board on the connection, it switches the ground position to that board's GPS for you."},{"t":"Ground station types","p":"","u":"ground-station-types.html","h":false,"b":"MPXAir can read the GPS of different ground stations that share the telemetry link. Pick yours and adjust its settings. Ground \u2192 Ground station type, with the board's details, the last message received, and the settings for this type."},{"t":"Choosing a type","p":"Ground station types","u":"ground-station-types.html#choosing-a-type","h":true,"b":"Select A ground station on the telemetry link as the ground position, then pick a Type : Type How the GPS arrives RFD900 + ESP32 ground station board MPXAir sends #GCS status once a second. The board replies with a #GCS {json} line that includes its GPS. See RFD900 + ESP32 . GPS mixed into the telemetry The ground station already sends NMEA sentences ( $GPGGA , $GNRMC \u2026) or MAVLink GPS messages on the link. Nothing is sent to it. MAVLink GPS from the ground station The ground station sends GPS_RAW_INT , GLOBAL_POSITION_INT or GPS_INPUT with its own system ID (255 by default). Custom board \u2014 JSON replies Your own firmware answers a text command with a JSON line, e.g. #GND {\"lat\":-35.36,\"lon\":149.16,\"alt\":584,\"sats\":11,\"fix\":3} . Custom board \u2014 comma-separated replies Your firmware sends lines such as $GND,-35.363261,149.165230,584.0,11,3 , on request or by itself. Building your own? See Connect your own ground station ."},{"t":"The settings for each type","p":"Ground station types","u":"ground-station-types.html#the-settings-for-each-type","h":true,"b":"Each type has its own settings, saved separately, so switching types never loses them. Change a value and press Save . Reset puts the defaults back. Test now sends the poll command once, and the reply appears under Raw message , which is the quickest way to check a new board. Above the settings, MPXAir shows what it understood from the board: the board (name, firmware, uptime), the GPS (fix, satellites, HDOP, altitude) and, for the RFD900 + ESP32 board, its radio links (RFD900 link quality, ELRS RSSI, LQ and SNR, RC rate) and board health (ESP32 temperature, bytes sent and received). Replies damaged by telemetry bytes mixed into the line are repaired where possible. Setting Meaning Poll command Text sent to ask for the status, e.g. #GCS status . Leave empty if the board sends its position by itself. Start command Sent once when the link opens (optional), e.g. #GCS hello . Reply prefix Replies start with this text, e.g. #GCS or $GND, . Everything after it is the data. Line ending Added after each command: \\r , \\n or \\r\\n . Poll every (seconds) How often the poll command is sent. Latitude / Longitude field For JSON, a path such as gps.lat . For comma-separated, the column number (the first value after the prefix is 1). Position scale 0.0000001 when the board sends degrees \u00d7 10\u2077, 1 for plain degrees. Altitude field / scale Altitude above sea level. Use scale 0.001 for millimetres, 1 for metres. Satellites, Fix, HDOP fields Optional. Fix can be true/false, or a number where 0 means no fix. Board name / Firmware field Optional, shown on the Ground tab. Only use replies that contain Ignore replies without this field (e.g. answers to other commands). Follow commands Optional commands that make the board broadcast its position to the aircraft. They switch on the Ground station sends its position to the aircraft switch. System ID (MAVLink types) Only use GPS messages from this MAVLink system. 0 = any system that isn't the aircraft. Release DTR and RTS Turn the serial control lines off when connecting. ESP32 boards need this or they may reset."},{"t":"Follow Me","p":"","u":"follow-me.html","h":false,"b":"Make the aircraft follow the ground station \u2014 either driven by MPXAir, or by the ground station board itself."},{"t":"Follow Me from MPXAir","p":"Follow Me","u":"follow-me.html#follow-me-from-mpxair","h":true,"b":"MPXAir sends the aircraft a new GUIDED target at your ground position, several times a second. Choose a moving ground position source: a ground station board, a USB GPS, or the computer's location. A fixed position won't work. On Ground \u2192 Follow Me , set the altitude above home , how far away to stay (0 = overhead), and how many updates per second. Press Start Follow Me and confirm. The aircraft switches to GUIDED and flies towards you. Follow Me pauses if the aircraft leaves GUIDED, so switching mode on your transmitter always takes back control. The status line shows what it's doing."},{"t":"ArduPilot Follow mode (board broadcast)","p":"Follow Me","u":"follow-me.html#ardupilot-follow-mode-board-broadcast","h":true,"b":"Ground station boards that can broadcast their own position (like the RFD900 + ESP32 ) can drive ArduPilot's built-in FOLLOW mode instead: On the aircraft, set FOLL_ENABLE = 1 and FOLL_SYSID = 255 (Parameters tab, group Follow mode (FOLL) ). On Ground \u2192 Ground station type , turn on Ground station sends its position to the aircraft . Switch the aircraft to FOLLOW mode. Safety Test Follow Me with plenty of space and a safe altitude, and keep your transmitter in hand."},{"t":"Antenna tracker","p":"","u":"antenna-tracker.html","h":false,"b":"Point a pan/tilt antenna at the aircraft automatically."},{"t":"What you need","p":"Antenna tracker","u":"antenna-tracker.html#what-you-need","h":true,"b":"A pan/tilt mount with two servos, driven by a Pololu Maestro servo controller or a serial pan/tilt board. A ground position. The tracker's position is the ground position, so use the ground station's GPS or type in a fixed position."},{"t":"Set it up","p":"Antenna tracker","u":"antenna-tracker.html#set-it-up","h":true,"b":"On Ground \u2192 Antenna tracker , choose the hardware: Pololu Maestro ; Serial pan/tilt (PWM) : the board receives !!!PAN:xxxx,TLT:xxxx lines in microseconds; Serial pan/tilt (degrees\u00d710) . Choose the serial port and baud rate, and the pan and tilt channels. Set the PWM minimum and maximum, the angle range each servo covers, and the trims. Reverse either servo if it moves the wrong way. Press Point north, level and check the antenna points north and level. Adjust the trims if not. Press Start tracking . The status line shows the aircraft's bearing, elevation and distance from the tracker. Mounts with more than 120\u00b0 of tilt flip over the top instead of spinning all the way round."},{"t":"RFD900 + ESP32 ground station","p":"","u":"rfd900-esp32.html","h":false,"b":"Using MPXAir with the RFD900 / ESP32-S3 router board and its on-board GPS."},{"t":"About the board","p":"RFD900 + ESP32 ground station","u":"rfd900-esp32.html#about-the-board","h":true,"b":"The board combines: an RFD900 telemetry radio, linked to the aircraft's RFD900; an ESP32-S3 that routes telemetry to USB and RC from an ELRS receiver to the aircraft; a GPS module wired to the ESP32 (UART 115200 baud on GPIO7/6, PPS on GPIO8). On the computer it appears as ttyACM0 \u00b7 Espressif (USB ID 303a ). Its own settings are changed with the board's GCS Settings page ."},{"t":"Connecting","p":"RFD900 + ESP32 ground station","u":"rfd900-esp32.html#connecting","h":true,"b":"Plug the board in and pick ACM0 \u00b7 Espressif at the top right. The ESP32's USB ignores the baud rate, so any value works. Press Connect . MPXAir releases DTR and RTS so the ESP32 isn't reset, and asks the board for its status. If the aircraft is on, it connects as usual. If not, the window says Ground station connected \u2014 waiting for the aircraft , and the ground GPS works straight away. The first time, the ground position switches to this board's GPS automatically. One program at a time The board's GCS Settings page and MPXAir can't use the USB port at the same time. Disconnect one before connecting the other."},{"t":"What MPXAir reads","p":"RFD900 + ESP32 ground station","u":"rfd900-esp32.html#what-mpxair-reads","h":true,"b":"Once a second MPXAir sends #GCS status . The board answers with a line starting #GCS { , mixed into the telemetry. MPXAir uses: Field Shown as gps.fix , gps.sats , gps.hdop Fix, satellites, HDOP gps.lat , gps.lon (degrees \u00d7 10\u2077), gps.altMm Ground position and altitude name , fw The board's name and firmware on the Ground tab follow Whether the board is broadcasting its position to the aircraft"},{"t":"Follow mode","p":"RFD900 + ESP32 ground station","u":"rfd900-esp32.html#follow-mode","h":true,"b":"Turn on Ground station sends its position to the aircraft (Ground tab). This sends #GCS follow 1 , and the board then broadcasts GLOBAL_POSITION_INT as system 255 twice a second. For ArduPilot's FOLLOW mode, set FOLL_ENABLE=1 and FOLL_SYSID=255 on the aircraft."},{"t":"Cold starts","p":"RFD900 + ESP32 ground station","u":"rfd900-esp32.html#cold-starts","h":true,"b":"The board's GPS has no backup battery, so every power-up is a cold start. Allow a few minutes with a clear view of the sky for the first fix."},{"t":"Connect your own ground station","p":"","u":"custom-ground-station.html","h":false,"b":"How to make any ground station with a GPS and a serial link to the computer work with MPXAir. Your ground station shares the USB/serial link with the aircraft telemetry (MAVLink). MPXAir picks your messages out of that stream, so you can send text lines between MAVLink packets, or MAVLink messages of your own. Choose whichever is easiest for your firmware, then pick the matching ground station type ."},{"t":"Rules for text lines","p":"Connect your own ground station","u":"custom-ground-station.html#rules-for-text-lines","h":true,"b":"Each line starts with a fixed prefix (for example #GND or $GND, ) and ends with \\n . Write the whole line at once, between MAVLink packets. Never insert it inside a MAVLink packet. Use plain ASCII and keep lines under a few kilobytes. MAVLink parsers skip text, so the aircraft link isn't disturbed."},{"t":"Option 1 \u2014 JSON reply to a command","p":"Connect your own ground station","u":"custom-ground-station.html#option-1-json-reply-to-a-command","h":true,"b":"MPXAir sends a poll command (default #GND? , then \\r\\n ) at the interval you choose. Your firmware answers with one line: #GND {\"lat\":-35.363261,\"lon\":149.165230,\"alt\":584.2,\"sats\":11,\"fix\":3,\"hdop\":0.8} Ground station type: Custom board \u2014 JSON replies . Set the field paths if yours differ (nested paths like gps.lat work). If you send degrees \u00d7 10\u2077, set the position scale to 0.0000001 . // Arduino / ESP32 sketch (uses the TinyGPSPlus library) #include <TinyGPSPlus.h> TinyGPSPlus gps; HardwareSerial GPSSerial(1); String cmd; void setup() { Serial.begin(115200); // USB link to the computer GPSSerial.begin(9600, SERIAL_8N1, 6, 7); // GPS module: RX, TX pins } void loop() { while (GPSSerial.available()) gps.encode(GPSSerial.read()); while (Serial.available()) { // (forward everything else to your radio here) char c = Serial.read(); if (c == '\\n' || c == '\\r') { if (cmd == \"#GND?\") { Serial.printf(\"#GND {\\\"lat\\\":%.7f,\\\"lon\\\":%.7f,\\\"alt\\\":%.1f,\\\"sats\\\":%u,\\\"fix\\\":%d,\\\"hdop\\\":%.1f}\\n\", gps.location.lat(), gps.location.lng(), gps.altitude.meters(), gps.satellites.value(), gps.location.isValid() ? 3 : 0, gps.hdop.hdop()); } cmd = \"\"; } else if (cmd.length() < 32) cmd += c; } }"},{"t":"Option 2 \u2014 comma-separated lines","p":"Connect your own ground station","u":"custom-ground-station.html#option-2-comma-separated-lines","h":true,"b":"The simplest firmware: print one line a second, without being asked. $GND,-35.363261,149.165230,584.2,11,3 Ground station type: Custom board \u2014 comma-separated replies . Columns: 1 latitude, 2 longitude, 3 altitude (m), 4 satellites, 5 fix. Change the column numbers if your order differs. Leave the poll command empty if the board sends lines by itself."},{"t":"Option 3 \u2014 forward the GPS's NMEA","p":"Connect your own ground station","u":"custom-ground-station.html#option-3-forward-the-gps-s-nmea","h":true,"b":"Pass the GPS module's $GPGGA / $GNGGA and $GPRMC / $GNRMC sentences straight into the USB stream (whole sentences, with their checksums). Ground station type: GPS mixed into the telemetry ."},{"t":"Option 4 \u2014 MAVLink","p":"Connect your own ground station","u":"custom-ground-station.html#option-4-mavlink","h":true,"b":"Send GPS_RAW_INT (24), GLOBAL_POSITION_INT (33) or GPS_INPUT (232) with a system ID other than the aircraft's. System 255 is the convention, and also works for ArduPilot's FOLLOW mode with FOLL_SYSID=255 . Ground station type: MAVLink GPS from the ground station , with the matching system ID."},{"t":"Tips","p":"Connect your own ground station","u":"custom-ground-station.html#tips","h":true,"b":"ESP32 boards can reset when DTR/RTS change. Keep Release DTR and RTS on for them. Use Test now and open Raw message on the Ground tab while you develop. MPXAir's built-in demo drone answers #GND? and #GCS status too, so you can compare against it."},{"t":"Testing with the ArduPilot simulator","p":"","u":"simulator.html","h":false,"b":"Fly a real ArduPilot copter, plane or rover on your computer to practise and test."},{"t":"Build SITL","p":"Testing with the ArduPilot simulator","u":"simulator.html#build-sitl","h":true,"b":"Follow ArduPilot's Linux build guide , then build the vehicle you want: cd ardupilot ./waf configure --board sitl ./waf copter"},{"t":"Run it","p":"Testing with the ArduPilot simulator","u":"simulator.html#run-it","h":true,"b":"./build/sitl/bin/arducopter --model quad --home -35.363261,149.165230,584,353 --defaults Tools/autotest/default_params/copter.parm -I0 In MPXAir choose TCP , enter 127.0.0.1:5760 and press Connect ."},{"t":"What to try","p":"Testing with the ArduPilot simulator","u":"simulator.html#what-to-try","h":true,"b":"Wait for GPS: 3D Fix (bottom-left of the map), then use Takeoff and Fly to here . Write a mission on Plan and press Start Mission . After landing, download and graph the log on Logs ."},{"t":"Troubleshooting","p":"","u":"troubleshooting.html","h":false,"b":"Fixes for the most common problems."},{"t":"Permission denied on a USB port","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"troubleshooting.html#permission-denied-on-a-usb-port","h":true,"b":"sudo usermod -aG dialout $USER Then log out and back in. install.sh offers to do this."},{"t":"A flight controller connects and then drops","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"troubleshooting.html#a-flight-controller-connects-and-then-drops","h":true,"b":"Ubuntu's ModemManager may be probing the device. Stop it: sudo systemctl disable --now ModemManager"},{"t":"A USB device disappears","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"troubleshooting.html#a-usb-device-disappears","h":true,"b":"If the device vanishes from lsusb and journalctl -k shows error -71 , device descriptor read or device not accepting address , the problem is electrical, not software: Try a different, short USB data cable and another USB port. A radio transmitting at high power can draw more current than a laptop port gives. Use external power or a powered USB hub. If a port stays stuck, restart the computer. journalctl -k -f # watch while plugging the device in"},{"t":"The ground station board keeps restarting","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"troubleshooting.html#the-ground-station-board-keeps-restarting","h":true,"b":"If journalctl -k shows USB disconnect for the board's hub every so often, and the board comes back a few seconds later (often as ttyACM1 ), the board is losing power for a moment (a brown-out). A radio transmitting at high power, the ESP32 and the GPS together can draw more than a laptop USB port supplies. Each brown-out also cold-starts a GPS without a backup battery, so it loses its satellites for a few minutes. Power the board from a powered USB hub or its own supply. Use a short, good-quality USB cable. MPXAir reconnects on its own when the board comes back, and keeps the last known ground position on the map meanwhile."},{"t":"Link quality around 95\u201398 %","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"troubleshooting.html#link-quality-around-95-98","h":true,"b":"A few damaged packets are normal on a radio link, and MAVLink is built to cope: MPXAir skips them and carries on. If you see a steady 2 % or more even at short range with a strong signal, check the radios' settings (on the RFD900 + ESP32 board, with its GCS Settings page): MAVLink framing ( MAVLINK = 1) on both radios, so the radio sends whole packets. Error correction ( ECC ) on both radios, with the same air speed and settings on each side. Power the ground board properly: power dips also corrupt data."},{"t":"PreArm: Vehicle breaching Polygon fence","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"troubleshooting.html#prearm-vehicle-breaching-polygon-fence","h":true,"b":"The aircraft is outside an inclusion geofence stored on it, or within FENCE_MARGIN metres of its edge. Look at the fence on the Fly map, move the aircraft well inside it, or edit or clear the fence on Plan \u2192 Geofence and press Write ."},{"t":"No heartbeat from the aircraft","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"troubleshooting.html#no-heartbeat-from-the-aircraft","h":true,"b":"Check the baud rate: 57600 for most telemetry radios, 115200 for USB. Check that both radios are powered and linked (the radio LEDs should be solid). With a ground station board, MPXAir waits for the aircraft and connects when it appears."},{"t":"The ground station GPS doesn't show","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"troubleshooting.html#the-ground-station-gps-doesn-t-show","h":true,"b":"On the Ground tab, open Raw message . If it's empty, the board isn't sending (try Test now , and check the prefix and poll command). A GPS without a backup battery needs a few minutes for its first fix. The GROUND marker can be hidden with the ground-station button on the Fly map."},{"t":"No map","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"troubleshooting.html#no-map","h":true,"b":"Check that Preferences \u2192 Maps \u2192 Internet use isn't set to Offline. Areas you downloaded with Offline Maps always work without internet."},{"t":"Some parameters are missing","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"troubleshooting.html#some-parameters-are-missing","h":true,"b":"On slow links a few parameters can be lost. The connection window says some missing . Press Refresh on the Parameters tab to try again."},{"t":"Reporting a problem","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"troubleshooting.html#reporting-a-problem","h":true,"b":"Open an issue on GitHub with what you did, what happened, and the terminal output if you started MPXAir from a terminal."},{"t":"Questions and answers","p":"","u":"faq.html","h":false,"b":"Short answers to common questions."},{"t":"Does MPXAir work with PX4?","p":"Questions and answers","u":"faq.html#does-mpxair-work-with-px4","h":true,"b":"No. MPXAir is made for ArduPilot (Copter, Plane, Rover, Sub, Antenna tracker)."},{"t":"Can I use my Mission Planner files?","p":"Questions and answers","u":"faq.html#can-i-use-my-mission-planner-files","h":true,"b":"Yes. MPXAir reads and writes .waypoints missions and .param files in Mission Planner's format, and it reads QGroundControl and MAVProxy parameter files."},{"t":"Does it need the internet?","p":"Questions and answers","u":"faq.html#does-it-need-the-internet","h":true,"b":"Only for map tiles, downloading firmware and updating parameter descriptions. Save map areas with Offline Maps before going to the field."},{"t":"Where are my files?","p":"Questions and answers","u":"faq.html#where-are-my-files","h":true,"b":"Telemetry recordings are in ~/Documents/MPXAir/telemetry-logs , downloaded logs in ~/Documents/MPXAir/flight-logs , settings in ~/.config/mpxair , and maps in ~/.cache/mpxair ."},{"t":"Can I run it from a USB stick?","p":"Questions and answers","u":"faq.html#can-i-run-it-from-a-usb-stick","h":true,"b":"Yes, with ./run.sh --portable . Everything is then kept in the MPXAir folder."},{"t":"Can I use it alongside another ground station?","p":"Questions and answers","u":"faq.html#can-i-use-it-alongside-another-ground-station","h":true,"b":"Not on the same serial port, because only one program can open it. Use UDP or TCP forwarding (for example from MAVProxy) to share a link."},{"t":"What's not in MPXAir yet?","p":"Questions and answers","u":"faq.html#what-s-not-in-mpxair-yet","h":true,"b":"Joystick control, a video feed, scripting, multiple vehicles at once, and some specialist setup screens. Those settings can still be changed on the Parameters tab."},{"t":"What's new","p":"","u":"changelog.html","h":false,"b":"Changes in each version of MPXAir."},{"t":"1.2.3","p":"What's new","u":"changelog.html#1-2-3","h":true,"b":"Disconnect asks for confirmation (with a stronger warning if the aircraft is armed), then closes the serial port completely and confirms it is free for other programs. While connected, MPXAir has the port to itself, so other programs get a clear \"busy\" error. If another program already has the port open, MPXAir names it. Fixed: the heartbeat could be written while the port was closing."},{"t":"1.2.2","p":"What's new","u":"changelog.html#1-2-2","h":true,"b":"Fixed: the firmware version was sometimes never learned on a busy radio link, so matching parameter descriptions weren't used. It's now requested until it arrives, and read from the startup banner as a fallback. Fixed: a step in the connection and Plan progress windows could stay on \"reading\u2026\". The GROUND label moves above its badge when the aircraft is right next to the ground station. mpxair --connect starts and connects to the last-used device."},{"t":"1.2.1","p":"What's new","u":"changelog.html#1-2-1","h":true,"b":"Fixed: a stray diagonal line on the HUD next to the pitch ladder; the ladder now covers \u00b190\u00b0 with dashed rungs below the horizon. Fixed: dropdowns showing no value when their first option was selected (e.g. Ground station type, parameter values). Fixed: the window opening wider than the screen, which hid the right-hand panels. Parameter descriptions matched to the aircraft's exact firmware version, a clearer description panel ( What it does , options), and descriptions in the list. Ground GPS card at the bottom-right of the Fly map. The GROUND marker stays at its last known position when the fix is lost. Clearer ground station panel (board, GPS, radio links, health), more robust reading of board replies. Automatic reconnect when a USB device drops off and comes back. \"3D Fix + DGPS\" instead of \"DGPS\", and a smaller map credit."},{"t":"1.2.0","p":"What's new","u":"changelog.html#1-2-0","h":true,"b":"Ground station types : RFD900 + ESP32, GPS in the telemetry (NMEA/MAVLink), MAVLink GPS from the ground station, and custom boards with JSON or comma-separated replies. Each type has its own editable settings, with Test now and the last message received. This documentation website."},{"t":"1.1.0","p":"What's new","u":"changelog.html#1-1-0","h":true,"b":"Connection and Plan read/write progress windows. Quick view with 2\u201316 tiles and 45 values. Parameter groups sidebar, and hover descriptions everywhere. Adjustable, auto-clearing flight trail, and the GPS card at the bottom-left of the map. Readable dropdowns and the Internet use choices."},{"t":"1.0.0","p":"What's new","u":"changelog.html#1-0-0","h":true,"b":"First public release: Fly, Plan, Setup, Parameters, Logs and Ground, the RFD900 + ESP32 board, offline maps, and the installer. Full details are in CHANGELOG.md ."}];
